<h1>How do I get hired when every job wants 2 years experience but I just graduated?</h1>

<p class="answer-capsule">The two-year requirement is mostly a filter, not a real need: employers write it to reduce application volume, then hire candidates who prove they can do the work anyway, so your move is not to wait two years but to build proof that overrides the requirement.</p>

<div class="tldr">
  <ul>
    <li>53% of employers have dropped degree requirements, up from 30% the year before, but fewer than 1 in 700 hires reflected real change, <a href="https://www.burningglassinstitute.org/research/skills-based-hiring-2024">Burning Glass Institute + HBS</a>. The gap between stated policy and actual hiring is where you operate.</li>
    <li>An internship cuts the odds of underemployment by 48.5%, <a href="https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6777c52f82e5471a3732ea25/679a6fadfda4220bbac585d7_Talent-Disrupted-2.pdf">Strada Institute + Burning Glass Institute</a>. If you skipped one, you need to replicate its function another way.</li>
    <li>Resume use fell to 67%, down from 73% in 2024, <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">TestGorilla 2025</a>. Recruiters are already looking past the document you are optimizing.</li>
    <li>New-grad hiring at big tech is down more than 50% from 2019; new grads are now only ~7% of hires, <a href="https://www.signalfire.com/blog/signalfire-state-of-talent-report-2025">SignalFire State of Talent Report 2025</a>. The posted jobs are not the whole market.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<section>
  <h2>Why do employers ask for two years they do not actually need?</h2>
  <p>The two-year floor is a heuristic, not a job analysis. Hiring managers inherit it from job-description templates, from HR systems that auto-populate requirements, and from the lazy assumption that time spent equals skill acquired. It functions as a noise filter: anyone desperate enough to apply anyway, or confident enough to ignore it, self-selects into a smaller pool the employer can actually review.</p>
  <p>Here is the honest part nobody puts in the posting. Most two-year roles involve tasks a competent graduate could execute in month one with proper onboarding. The real risk employers fear is not your missing months. It is their own broken onboarding, their lack of time to train, and the possibility that you will leave before they recoup the investment. The experience requirement transfers that risk onto you.</p>
  <p>Your response is not to argue with the posting. It is to enter through a side door with evidence that dissolves the objection before it forms: a portfolio piece that mirrors the company's actual work, a referral from someone inside who can vouch for your execution, or a scored simulation that demonstrates the specific skill in question, each of which bypasses the time-based filter the same way a skills-based assessment bypasses a degree requirement.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Where does real entry-level hiring actually happen?</h2>
  <p>The public job board is the tip of a submerged market. Internal referrals, university recruiting pipelines, and hidden backfill roles account for a massive share of actual placements, especially for candidates without the requested experience. The SignalFire data on collapsed new-grad hiring at big tech does not mean no one is hiring graduates. It means the traditional channels, the ones you were told to use, are shrinking.</p>
  <p>The growth is elsewhere. Mid-size companies without formal campus programs often need talent more urgently than they need credentials. Startups at Series B or C, where the founding team is overwhelmed and the HR function is thin, frequently write "2 years experience" because they copied a template, then hire the first person who demonstrates they can ship. The trick is finding them before the posting goes live, or bypassing the posting entirely.</p>
  <p>This is where the degree-as-mortgage analogy bites. You were sold the idea that the credential itself would open doors through established pipelines. The pipeline is clogged. The value now is in your ability to route around it, to find the hiring manager on LinkedIn, to send work that matches their current project, to get introduced by someone who already proved themselves inside. The network you built, or failed to build, matters more than the line items on your resume.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>What replaces the experience you do not have?</h2>
  <p>Employers who say "2 years" often mean "I need evidence you will not need hand-holding." The evidence can take several forms, and none require calendar time in a previous role.</p>
  <p>First, <strong>scoped work in public</strong>. A marketing graduate who runs paid acquisition for a friend's Shopify store, documents the results, and can speak to CAC and ROAS has more relevant signal than a year of generic "marketing coordinator" tasks at a company no one recognizes. The work should be specific, measured, and attached to a real business outcome, even a small one.</p>
  <p>Second, <strong>simulation or scored proof</strong>. The shift toward skills-based hiring, however incomplete in practice, means some employers now accept demonstrations in place of tenure. A scored submission against a professional bar, with feedback that shows iterative improvement, functions as proxy experience, and while it is not the same as two years in a role, for many positions it is sufficient to get the interview, which is where the requirement dissolves.</p>
  <p>Third, <strong>internship function without the internship title</strong>. The Strada data on internship impact is stark: the experience itself, not the credential, drives the employment outcome. If you missed the formal window, replicate the components with real client work, even pro bono, a short-term project with a startup, or a case competition with deliverables that mirror professional standards. The goal is not the line on your resume but the story you tell in the interview about solving a problem under constraints.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>How do you get the first interview without the filter letting you through?</h2>
  <p>The application portal is a trap designed to reject you. ATS systems parse for keywords, and "2 years experience" is a keyword you fail by definition. The manual override comes from a human who already knows your work.</p>
  <p>The most reliable path is <strong>warm introduction to the hiring manager</strong>, not to HR. Find the person who would actually manage the role. Study their recent work, their public posts, their company's current challenges. Send not a resume but a brief note with a specific observation and a piece of work that demonstrates the skill in question. This is labor-intensive and scales poorly, which is precisely why it works, since most graduates mass-apply instead.</p>
  <p>The second path is <strong>contract or project-to-hire entry</strong>. Many employers will test you on a discrete task with lower risk than a full hire: a two-week project, a freelance deliverable, a part-time trial. This converts to permanent roles at rates far above cold application success, and it gives you the experience line for the next role. The internship-to-full-time conversion rate of 63.1% works because the employer has already seen you perform, so manufacture that dynamic yourself.</p>
  <p>The third path, slower but compounding, is <strong>visibility in the spaces where your target employers already look</strong>. Write about their industry with specificity. Build in public so your work appears when they search for solutions to problems they have. The resume is dying, per the TestGorilla data, but discoverability is rising, so be the person they find before they post the job.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>What do you do if you are six months out and still stuck?</h2>
  <p>The gap itself becomes the new filter. Employers who were willing to consider fresh graduates now see someone who graduated and failed to land, which reads as signal of lower quality. You must interrupt that narrative with evidence of continued growth.</p>
  <p>The wrong move is more applications with the same resume. The right move is a visible project completed during the gap, with specificity that shows you were not waiting: a data analysis of public datasets relevant to your target industry, a rebuilt website for a local business, a content series that demonstrates domain thinking. The content of the work matters less than the fact that it is dated, public, and shows you were active and improving while others assumed you were stagnant.</p>
  <p>Consider also whether your target is too narrow. The big-tech collapse in new-grad hiring means competition at the top is ferocious, while roles at growing companies in unglamorous industries go unfilled. A first role in industrial software, B2B services, or regional tech builds the experience line that unlocks the sexier name later. The degree is a mortgage you already paid; do not insist on extracting value from the exact lender you imagined.</p>
</section>

<section class="faq">
  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>Should I apply to jobs that say 2 years experience if I have none?</h3>
    <p>Yes, but not through the front door. Direct application through ATS will likely auto-reject. Use the posting to identify the hiring manager, then reach directly with proof of relevant skill. The requirement is a filter, not a hard wall.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>Does a master's degree count as the two years?</h3>
    <p>Rarely in practice. Most employers mean professional execution, not additional academic time. A master's can help in specific technical or research roles, but for generalist positions it often adds debt without addressing the real gap, which is proof of work.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>How do I get referrals if I do not know anyone in the industry?</h3>
    <p>Start with second-degree connections: alumni from your university, people who share your hometown or interests, participants in online communities where your target industry congregates. The ask is not "refer me to a job." It is a specific question about their work, followed by demonstration that you have done yours.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>Is lying about experience ever worth it?</h3>
    <p>No. Background checks are routine, and the risk of termination with cause is not worth the marginal gain. The better strategy is reframing: contract work, volunteer projects, and intensive self-directed builds all constitute real experience if you can speak to them specifically and honestly.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>How long should I spend building proof versus applying?</h3>
    <p>If your current application rate is yielding no interviews, the ratio is wrong. A week of targeted outreach with one strong portfolio piece beats a month of mass application. Build until you have something that makes a hiring manager pause, then apply selectively with that asset as the opener.</p>
  </div>
</section>

